!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/35/ltr8/http/meta-http-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
@doc:"""
  A meta layer adding HTTP operations to the type system: a schema governed by this one describes an
  HTTP API, and its request and response payloads are type references the compiler resolves.

  This is the design picked out of four explored side by side; the other three, and the comparison,
  are in git history. What this one has that a data document cannot is the reference: `body: order`
  is checked when the description loads, where a data description spells "order" as a string and
  needs its own resolver -- forty lines that reimplemented an upstream namespace bug twice before
  they were right.

  `data &` is the base kind for an entry that is not a type: an operation describes an endpoint,
  and no data value ever has one as its type. §4.1 declares that kind and gives this exact
  composition as its worked example -- an HTTP operation binding request and response types by name
  is what it calls the motivating case -- and makes naming a DATA-kinded entry where a type is
  expected a resolver error, so the misuse is caught at schema load rather than at read.

  Composing with `data` is itself the permission for a SCHEMA to write `!operation { ... }`
  (§3.3.1/§5.6), and it is not a claim that the thing is a type. What makes an entry a constructor
  is that it IS-A `top` (§4.1) -- it composes, transitively, with a base kind -- which the supertype
  chain records, so no separate marker asserts it. Declaring one is permitted only in a
  meta-kernel-governed schema, which is why this document's own !!meta is the kernel.

  request/response are `type_ref`, not `type_name` or text, and §9 makes that a MUST for an extension
  meta-schema: `type_ref` is the slot that participates in reference walking and structural identity,
  where a `type_name` slot carries a bare token the resolver treats as data, invisible to
  every reference mechanism. Concretely here, a Data body hands its references to the linker, so a
  name that resolves to nothing is an author error at schema load.

  Governed directly by the meta-kernel, which is not a style choice: declaring a constructor is
  permitted only in a schema whose own !!meta is the kernel. So this is a SIBLING of meta.tn, and
  imports it for the vocabulary its own declarations use.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  parameter_location => !enum [PATH QUERY HEADER]

  @doc:"""
    A parameter carried outside the body. `type` names a scalar and nothing here enforces that -- a URL
    segment cannot carry a record, and the type system has no way to say so.

    `description` is a field rather than an @doc because a parameter is a VALUE inside an operation's
    payload, not a schema entry, so there is nothing for an annotation to attach to. Same for response.
    """
  parameter => {
    name:        text
    in:          parameter_location
    type:        type_ref
    required:    boolean
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"One response. `body` is absent for a 204, or a document served as bytes."
  response => {
    status:      status_code
    body:        type_ref?
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One operation. A list of responses rather than a map keyed by status: a response is likely to
    grow members beyond its body, and a map-typed slot cannot hold a template parameter, which would
    foreclose templated operations before they are tried.

    There is no `description` field: an operation IS a schema entry, so @doc carries its long form and
    a reader gets it back from the entry's annotations. `summary` is the short form OpenAPI keeps
    beside the long one.

    Worth knowing, because getting it wrong looks exactly like a library bug: a schema entry has TWO
    annotation positions and they land in different places. `@doc:"..." op => ...` annotates the ENTRY and is read
    from the entries map's own annotations; `op => @doc:"..." { ... }` annotates the DEFINITION and is
    read from the TypeDefinition. Both are retained. Looking only at the second and concluding @doc was
    dropped is the mistake.

    The entry's NAME is the operationId, and unlike OpenAPI's it lives in a namespace with a collision
    rule, so two operations cannot quietly share one.

    Deliberately absent, each a decision rather than an oversight: security schemes, response headers,
    links, callbacks, examples, tags, servers, and multi-media-type content negotiation. Adding one is
    meta-http-2.tn under §3.5 once this is published, never an edit.
    """
  operation => data & {
    method:      http_method
    path:        text
    summary:     text?
    deprecated:  boolean?
    parameters:  [parameter]?
    request:     type_ref?
    responses:   [response]
  }
}
